'''backrack†''', '''backrag†''', ''n.'' See ''Bacharach.''

'''back-racket†''' (bak′rak″et), ''n.'' The return of a ball in tennis; hence, figuratively, a countercharge.
{{smaller block|''Hoa.'' Why, are not debts better than words, sir?
''Wit.'' Are not words promises, and are not promises debts, sir?
''Hoa.'' He plays at ''back-racket'' with me.
{{right|''Middleton'', Trick to Catch the Old One, iv. 4.}}}}

'''back-raking''' (bak′rā″king), ''n.'' In ''farriery'', an operation by which hardened fæces are withdrawn from the rectum.

'''back-rent''' (bak′rent), ''n.'' '''1.''' Arrears of rent.—'''2.''' In ''Scots law'', a rent paid subsequently to reaping. {{smaller|Thus, when a tenant entering with a lease is allowed to reap and sell his first crop before paying his rent, the rent in this case is termed a ''back-rent'', in contradistinction to ''fore-rent'', a rent payable before the first crop is reaped.}}

'''back-rest''' (bak′rest), ''n.'' A guide attached to the slide-rest of a lathe and placed in contact with the work to steady it in turning.

'''back-return†''' (bak′rẹ̄-tėrn″), ''n.'' A going or coming back; return.
{{smaller block|Harry’s ''back-return'' again to France.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., v. (cho).}}}}
{{smaller block|The ''back-return'' of Charon’s boat. {{right|''Marlowe.''}}}}

'''backrope''' (bak′rōp), ''n.'' ''Naut.'': (''a'') The rope or chain-stay extending from the lower end of the dolphin-striker to each side of the bows of a ship. (''b'') A small rope attached to the hook of the cat-block or fish-hook, to facilitate hook-

